==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=always|:inh|enm:ay<id:always>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|ay}}, {{m|enm|ai}}, {{m|enm|aȝȝ}}, from {{der|en|non|ei}}, {{m|non|ey}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*aiwa}}, {{m|gem-pro|*aiwą}}{{,}} and/or {{m|gem-pro|*aiwō||ever, always}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*aiwaz||age; law}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂eyu-||long time}}. {{doublet|en|aeviternity|aevum}}.

See also {{cog|ang|āwo}}, {{m|ang|āwa}}, {{m|ang|ā}}, {{m|ang|ō}}, {{cog|dum|ie}}, {{cog|de|je}}; also {{cog|ang|ǣ|ǣ(w)|law}}, {{cog|fy|ieu||century}}, {{cog|nl|eeuw||century}}; also {{cog|ga|aois||age, period}}, {{cog|br|oad||age, period}}, {{cog|la|aevum||eternity}}, {{cog|grc|αἰών}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|ay}}
* {{alter|en|oy}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/eɪ/}}
** {{rhymes|en|eɪ|s=1}}
** {{hmp|en|A}}
* {{a|en|sometimes proscribed}}<ref>{{R:OED2}}</ref> {{IPA|en|/aɪ/}}
** {{rhymes|en|aɪ|s=1}}
** {{homophones|en|ay|eye|I}}

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[ever|Ever]], [[always]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|IV|i|page=15|column=2|passage={{...}}Do that good miſcheefe, which may make this Iſland / Thine owne for euer, and I thy ''{{w|Caliban}}'', / For '''aye''' thy foot-licker.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1834|author=w:Samuel Taylor Coleridge|title=The Rime of the Ancient Mariner
|passage=The ship drove fast, loud roared the blast, / And southward '''aye''' we fled.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=Wheelbarrow|pages=65–66|pageref=66|passage=Huge hills and mountains of casks on casks were piled upon her wharves, and side by side the world-wandering whale ships lay silent and safely moored at last; while from others came a sound of carpenters and coopers, with blended noises of fires and forges to melt the pitch, all betokening that new cruises were on the start; that one most perilous and long voyage ended, only begins a second; and a second ended, only begins a third, and so on, for ever and for '''aye'''.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author={{w|Catherine Winkworth}} (translator)|title={{w|Praise to the Lord, the Almighty}}|year=1863|text=Let the Amen sound from His people again; / Gladly for '''aye''' we adore Him.}}

