===Etymology 2===
{{ety|en|id=yes}}
"Appears suddenly about 1575, and is exceedingly common about 1600."<ref name="NED">{{R:New English Dictionary|volume=I|page=601|column=3}}</ref> Probably from use of {{m|en|aye|id=always|t=ever, always}} as expression of agreement or affirmation, or from {{der|en|enm|a ye||oh yes}}, or synthesis of both. Compare {{cog|fo|ája|t=certainly, ah yes}}. More at {{l|en|oh}}, {{l|en|yea}}. ''{{w|Online Etymology Dictionary}}'' also with these posits a possible descent from {{m|en|I}} (as if clipped from e.g. "I assent").

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|ay}}
* {{alter|en|oy}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/aɪ/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-aye.wav|a=Southern England}}
** {{rhymes|en|aɪ|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|ay|eye|I}}

====Interjection====
{{en-intj}}

# [[yes|Yes]]; [[yea]]; {{n-g|a word expressing [[assent]], or an [[affirmative]] answer to a question.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida Q1|act=I|scene=i|sig=A2|page=9|passage=[[w:Troilus|''Tro''{{quote-gloss|''ylus''}}]]. Haue I not tarried? [[w:Pandarus|''Pan''{{quote-gloss|''darus''}}]]. '''I''' the grinding; but you muſt tarry the boulting. ''Troy''. Haue I not tarried? ''Paude''.{{sic}} '''I''' the boulting; but you muſt tarry the leauening. ''Troy''. Still haue I tarried. ''Pan''. '''I''', to the leauening, but heares yet in the word hereafter, the kneading, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1925|month=spring|author=[[w:D. H. Lawrence|D{{quote-gloss|avid}} H{{quote-gloss|erbert}} Lawrence]]|chapter_plain=scene VII|title=David: A Play|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Alfred A. Knopf|Alfred A{{quote-gloss|braham}} Knopf]]|year_published=1926|page=46|pageurl=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015025813414&seq=58|oclc=5243065|passage={{smallcaps|david:}} {{...}} Will he surely give him his daughter? The daughter of his house in Gilgal? {{smallcaps|soldier:}} '''Aye''', surely he will.}}
# {{sid|en|acknowledge}} {{lb|en|nautical}} {{n-g|A word used to acknowledge a command from a superior, usually preceded by a [[verbatim]] repeat-back.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* It is much used in [[w:Scottish English|Scotland]], the [[w:Northern English|north]] and [[w:English language in England#Midlands|Midlands]] of England, Northern Ireland, and North Wales, as well as in New Zealand (where it may follow rather than precede a statement). Also notably seen in {{m|en|viva voce}} voting in legislative bodies, etc., or in [[nautical]] contexts.

